In this lesson we'll look at how calculus shows up on motion graphs. Picture a particle moving along a straight line, and you've got a graph of how far it is from a starting point versus time — that's called the displacement-time graph. The steepness of that graph tells you the speed. If the graph is flat, the object is stationary. And the exact mathematical way to get that steepness is by differentiating displacement with respect to time, which gives you velocity. Now, take that velocity and differentiate it again, and you get acceleration, which is the steepness of the velocity-time graph. On a velocity-time graph, the height of the curve is the speed at that moment, while the slope tells you how quickly the speed is changing. A flat velocity graph means constant velocity, so acceleration is zero. If the velocity line crosses through zero, that means the object is changing direction at that instant — it's not speeding up or slowing down overall, it's just turning around. One last thing — when you find the points where velocity equals zero, those show up as turning points on the displacement graph, moments where the object is momentarily still. Now let's see it in action.